README file for vapi-conduit
================================
Paul H. Hargrove <PHHargrove@lbl.gov>

Notes for myself for extended API:

+ The send completion facility consists of two pointers to counters,
  associated with each sbuf.  If these pointers are non-NULL then the
  counter is decremented atomically when the send is complete.
  
  One counter is for awaiting reuse of local memory and is
  only be used for sbufs which are doing zero copy.  This counter
  provides the mechanism for Longs and non-bulk puts to block before
  they return, and sould be allocated as an automatic variable.

  The second counter is for remote completion and should be non-NULL
  for every sbuf for which remote completion would be checked (all
  puts, but not the Longs).  For the nb and nbi the counter should
  be waited on at sync-time.  This means that the explicit handle is
  probably just the address of a counter (or a struct containing one).
  
  DONE

+ Similar to the reference implementation's cut-off between Mediums
  (which typically do a source-side copy) and Longs (which may not),
  we have a cut-off size, below which the RDMA-put operation will do
  source-side copies IFF local completion is desired (Long, put_nb,
  and put_nbi).

  DONE

+ The gets will be done w/ RDMA-reads, and will use the bounce buffers
  associated with the sbufs if the local memory is not in the segment
  (or otherwise registered).  The value gets will also go though the
  bounce buffers.  Clearly there is no bulk/non-bulk distinction in
  terms of local memory reuse, just the alignment and optimal size
  distinctions.  So, only the "remote" counter on the sbuf is needed
  for synchronization in all cases.

+ The memset and barrier routines will continue to be AM-based.

+ Table of when syncronization is needed
	Operation     Local Remote
	--------------------------
	LongAsync       X     X
	Long            I     X

	put_nb          I     S
	put_nbi         I     S
	put_nb_bulk     X     S
	put_nbi_bulk    X     S
	put_nb_val	X     S
	put_nbi_val	X     S
	put             X     I
	put_bulk        X     I
	put_val         X     I

	get_nb		X     S
	get_nbi		X     S
	get_nb_bulk	X     S
	get_nbi_bulk	X     S
	get_nb_val	X     S
	get_nbi_val (DOES NOT EXIST)
	get		X     I
	get_bulk	X     I
	get_val		X     I

   X = Not needed at all (or not even applicable with _val forms)
   I = Needed before (I)nitiating function returns
   S = Needed before (S)ynchronizing function returns

+ Some minor tweeks are possible to avoid allocation of counters in
  some cases.  There should be a small set of core functions (perhaps
  just the existing rdma_put, plus an rdma_get) which take pointers to
  the counters as arguments.
  - For all the functions which require waiting on a counter in in the
    initiating function, the counter can be allocated on the stack (as
    an automatic variable).
  - For the implicit-handle forms the remote counter is in
    thread-specific data, possibly in an access-region.
  - For the explicit handle forms the remote counter must be allocated
    from some pool, requiring some memory management work.  It may be
    advantageous to make the pools thread-local to avoid some locks.
